        'On-Campus vs. Off-Campus Housing' => <<<'HTML'
<p>On-campus housing trades some independence for proximity and a campus-managed experience. Off-campus housing can provide more choice, but it makes you a tenant responsible for a separate lease, utilities, transportation, and local housing rules. Compare the full academic-year cost and obligations, not just the advertised monthly price.</p>

<h2>Compare the same time period</h2>
<p>A residence-hall charge may cover a semester or academic year, while an apartment lease may run 10, 12, or more months. Convert both choices to the same dates. Include application and administrative fees, deposits, utilities, internet, furniture, renters insurance, laundry, parking, commuting, moving, storage, and food. Check whether financial aid arrives before each bill is due.</p>

<h2>Understand each contract</h2>
<p>Campus housing is governed by a housing agreement and student rules; it is not necessarily the same as a residential lease. The agreement may address enrollment, room changes, closures, meal plans, guests, breaks, conduct, and early termination. An off-campus lease is a legal contract with a landlord. School withdrawal usually does not cancel it. Read both contracts before paying a nonrefundable charge.</p>

<h2>Consider time and access</h2>
<p>Map the actual trip to early classes, labs, work, groceries, healthcare, and evening activities. Test transit frequency, parking availability, winter or severe-weather conditions, disability access, and late-night options. A cheaper apartment can cost more after parking, fuel, fares, rideshares, and lost time.</p>

<h2>Compare daily life</h2>
<p>On campus may offer resident staff, furnished rooms, maintenance, events, and easier social contact, but less privacy and more community rules. Off campus may offer kitchens, quieter space, partner or family housing, and neighborhood choice, but you may need to arrange furniture, repairs, utilities, cleaning, and roommate replacement. Verify what is actually included.</p>

<h2>Check eligibility and accessibility</h2>
<p>Some schools require certain students to live on campus or offer limited housing during breaks. Ask about exemptions and deadlines. For disability-related housing needs, contact campus accessibility or housing early. Off campus, federal fair-housing protections prohibit covered discrimination based on race, color, national origin, religion, sex, familial status, and disability; state and local law may cover more characteristics.</p>

<h2>Protect your finances</h2>
<p>Ask financial aid how each option fits the school’s cost-of-attendance budget. Aid eligibility does not mean the school will pay an off-campus landlord directly. Build an emergency reserve for deposits, utility setup, repairs, or a roommate who pays late. Do not borrow up to the maximum merely because housing is included in the aid budget.</p>

<h2>Decision checklist</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Calculate the total cost for identical dates.</li>
  <li>Read cancellation, renewal, guest, pet, parking, and subletting terms.</li>
  <li>Tour the room or unit and test the commute.</li>
  <li>Confirm who handles repairs, safety, accessibility, and emergencies.</li>
  <li>Keep a written backup plan if housing, a roommate, or enrollment changes.</li>
</ol>

<hr><h2>Official sources</h2>
<ul>
