        'Finding Your First College Apartment' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Your first apartment search should begin with a maximum total monthly cost, required move-in date, and list of nonnegotiable needs. Start early enough to compare units without sending money under pressure. A listing is an advertisement, not proof that the unit exists, is available, or may legally be rented by the person posting it.</p>

<h2>Build a realistic budget</h2>
<p>Set a limit for rent plus utilities, internet, renters insurance, parking, transportation, laundry, and household supplies. Add application fees, a security deposit, utility deposits, moving costs, and basic furnishings. Ask whether rent is quoted per person, per bedroom, or for the entire unit and whether a “student” installment plan still creates a fixed-term obligation.</p>

<h2>Choose location by routine</h2>
<p>Map classes, work, groceries, healthcare, and evening commitments. Visit at the times you would travel, not only during a scheduled tour. Check transit schedules, parking permits, bike storage, sidewalks, lighting, noise, flood or weather exposure, cell service, and accessibility. Ask the school whether it maintains an off-campus housing list, but confirm whether listings are screened or merely posted.</p>

<h2>Verify the listing and landlord</h2>
<p>Search the address, owner or management company, and phone number independently. Compare duplicate listings for different prices or contacts. Tour the exact unit when possible and verify the agent through the company’s official website. For a private owner, local property or tax records may help confirm ownership. Never rely only on a self-guided tour or copied video.</p>

<h2>Inspect before applying</h2>
<p>Look for working locks, windows, smoke and carbon-monoxide alarms where required, water pressure, leaks, pests, heating and cooling, outlets, appliances, ventilation, and signs of damage. Ask who controls utilities and repairs. Photograph only with permission and record promised repairs in writing. Do not assume a model unit matches the unit you will receive.</p>

<h2>Understand screening</h2>
<p>Landlords may request income, rental, credit, or background information subject to law. Ask what criteria, fees, guarantor rules, and documents apply before sharing sensitive data. If a tenant-screening report causes a denial, higher deposit, or required cosigner, federal law generally requires an adverse-action notice identifying the reporting company and explaining how to request and dispute the report.</p>

<h2>Read before paying</h2>
<p>Request the complete proposed lease and all addenda. Review dates, total rent, utilities, deposits, fees, joint liability, guests, pets, maintenance, entry, renewal, subletting, early termination, and move-out rules. Confirm every promise in the signed documents. Avoid payment methods that are hard to reverse, and obtain a receipt.</p>

<h2>Before accepting keys</h2>
<p>Complete a detailed move-in condition report with dated photos or video, activate utilities, obtain renters insurance if required or useful, and confirm emergency maintenance. Store the lease, inspection, receipts, and management contact somewhere every signer can access.</p>

