        'Understanding a Lease as a Student' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A lease is a binding rental contract. Being a student, changing schools, graduating early, losing a roommate, or leaving for summer does not ordinarily erase the obligation unless the lease or applicable law provides a way out. Read the complete lease and every addendum before signing or paying.</p>

<h2>Identify the parties and premises</h2>
<p>Confirm the legal landlord or management company, every tenant and guarantor, the exact unit or bedroom, included parking and storage, furnishings, and access to shared areas. In a by-the-bedroom lease, each tenant may have a separate obligation. In a joint lease, language such as “joint and several liability” may let the landlord pursue one signer for amounts another roommate failed to pay.</p>

<h2>Calculate the full obligation</h2>
<p>Record the start and end dates, total rent, installment schedule, grace period, permitted payment methods, late charges, utilities, recurring fees, deposit, and move-in charges. A lease advertised as “12 installments” may not be month-to-month. Ask which charges are refundable and whether concessions must be repaid after default or early departure.</p>

<h2>Read the use and conduct rules</h2>
<p>Review occupancy, guests, subletting, pets, smoking, noise, parking, alterations, business use, and community rules. Determine whether rules can change during the term. If an assistance animal or other disability-related accommodation is needed, use the housing provider’s reasonable-accommodation process; do not assume a pet clause answers that request.</p>

<h2>Understand repairs and entry</h2>
<p>The lease should explain how to report maintenance and emergencies, which tasks belong to the tenant, and how management enters the unit. State and local law may impose additional habitability, notice, repair, or anti-retaliation requirements. Report conditions promptly through the required channel and save photos, tickets, and written responses.</p>

<h2>Plan for change</h2>
<p>Read assignment, subletting, roommate replacement, military, domestic-violence, disability, early-termination, and lease-break provisions. Some rights come from law and are location- or fact-specific. Never assume finding a replacement releases you; obtain the landlord’s written approval and a written release if one is granted.</p>

<h2>Know renewal and move-out terms</h2>
<p>Find the notice deadline for nonrenewal, automatic renewal language, holdover rate, cleaning standard, inspection procedure, key-return instructions, and forwarding-address requirement. Calendar deadlines immediately. Continue paying and following the lease while disputing an issue unless reliable local legal advice says another remedy is lawful.</p>

<h2>Before signing</h2>
<p>Make sure blanks are filled, promises are written, all attachments are present, and every signer receives the same final copy. Seek campus legal services, local legal aid, or a licensed attorney if you do not understand liability, a guaranty, or a waiver. A roommate agreement helps roommates but normally does not change what tenants owe the landlord.</p>

<hr><h2>Official reference points</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://www.usa.gov/tenant-rights">USAGov: state tenant-rights resources</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.hud.gov/stat/fheo/rights-obligations">HUD: fair-housing rights and obligations</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/rules-policy/tenant-background-checks/review-your-rental-background-check/">Consumer Financial Protection Bureau: rental background checks</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. This is general information, not legal advice. The signed lease and applicable state, local, and federal law control.</em></p>
