        'Security Deposits Explained' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A security deposit is money held to secure obligations under a lease, commonly unpaid rent or tenant-caused damage. It is different from an application fee, holding deposit, first month’s rent, pet fee, or nonrefundable move-in charge. Labels do not always determine legal treatment, and rules vary significantly by state, locality, and housing program.</p>

<h2>Ask what each payment is</h2>
<p>Before paying, request an itemized move-in statement showing the amount, purpose, refundability, recipient, and conditions. Confirm whether a holding payment becomes part of the deposit or is lost if you do not sign. Pay the verified landlord or manager using an agreed traceable method and retain a receipt.</p>

<h2>Check local limits and handling rules</h2>
<p>State or local law may cap a deposit, require a separate account, mandate interest, regulate additional pet deposits, or impose a deadline for return and itemization. Other locations may not. Use an official state housing agency, attorney general, court self-help center, or legal-aid source for the property’s location rather than a generic internet chart.</p>

<h2>Document move-in condition</h2>
<p>Complete the landlord’s inspection form before its deadline. Take dated photos or video of every room, appliance, wall, floor, window, fixture, meter, key, and existing problem. Send the completed report through a method that creates proof of delivery and keep the original files. Report safety and repair concerns separately; a condition form is not always a maintenance request.</p>

<h2>Protect the deposit during the tenancy</h2>
<p>Report leaks, pests, broken equipment, or other problems promptly so damage does not grow. Get written permission before painting, mounting equipment, changing locks, adding a pet, or making alterations. Keep rent and utility records. Roommates should decide in writing how the deposit is divided, but the landlord will follow the lease and law, not necessarily the roommates’ private split.</p>

<h2>Prepare for move-out</h2>
<p>Read the required notice, cleaning, inspection, repair, key-return, and forwarding-address rules. Request a pre-move-out inspection where available. Remove property, photograph the empty unit, record meter readings when relevant, return every key as instructed, and get confirmation of possession. “Normal wear and tear” and charge standards depend on local law and facts.</p>

<h2>If money is withheld</h2>
<p>Compare the itemized deductions and return date with the lease and local law. Ask for supporting invoices or documentation where permitted. Dispute specific charges in writing with your move-in and move-out evidence. If unresolved, options may include mediation, a state consumer or housing agency, legal aid, or small-claims court. Deadlines apply.</p>

<h2>Avoid common mistakes</h2>
<p>Do not treat the deposit as the last month’s rent unless the landlord and law expressly allow it. Do not assume one roommate’s departure triggers a partial refund. Do not send a deposit before verifying the property, the person renting it, and the complete written terms.</p>

<hr><h2>Official reference points</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://www.usa.gov/tenant-rights">USAGov: tenant rights by state</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/rental-listing-scams">Federal Trade Commission: rental payment scams</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.usa.gov/state-consumer">USAGov: state consumer-protection offices</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. This is general information, not legal advice. Deposit definitions, caps, deductions, interest, and return deadlines are location-specific.</em></p>
