        'Avoiding Student Housing Scams' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Rental scammers copy legitimate listings, invent unavailable units, impersonate owners or agents, and collect application fees, deposits, rent, or identity information. Students searching from another city and under deadline pressure are attractive targets. Verify the property and the person before sending documents or money.</p>

<h2>Recognize pressure and payment red flags</h2>
<p>Be cautious when rent is far below comparable units, the contact refuses a live or in-person viewing, claims to be abroad, rushes payment, communicates only through messaging, or will not provide a complete lease. The FTC warns that demands for wire transfers, gift cards, cryptocurrency, or similarly hard-to-reverse payments are strong scam signs.</p>

<h2>Verify the listing independently</h2>
<p>Search the full address and images. Compare prices, contact names, and descriptions across sites. Find the management company’s official website yourself and call its published number. For a private landlord, check local property or tax records and identification where lawful. A lockbox or self-guided tour proves access, not authority to rent the unit.</p>

<h2>Inspect the exact housing</h2>
<p>Visit the actual unit when possible, or ask a trusted local person to visit. A live video tour should show the exterior, unit number, current interior, and requested details, but it is not a guarantee. Ask campus off-campus housing staff what verification they perform; a school-affiliated bulletin board may still contain third-party listings.</p>

<h2>Protect personal information</h2>
<p>Ask why each application item is needed, how it will be protected, and whether the person requesting it is verified. Do not send identity documents or Social Security, bank, payroll, or credit information through an unverified link. A scammer may seek identity data even without collecting a deposit. Use unique passwords and multifactor authentication for rental portals.</p>

<h2>Read the contract and payment instructions</h2>
<p>Get the complete lease, addenda, address, parties, dates, total charges, and refund terms before paying. Confirm the payment recipient matches the verified owner or manager. Use a payment method with records and available protections; keep the advertisement, messages, lease, receipt, and transaction details.</p>

<h2>If you already paid or shared data</h2>
<p>Contact the bank, card issuer, payment service, or wire company immediately and ask whether the transaction can be stopped or reversed. Change exposed passwords, monitor accounts, and use IdentityTheft.gov if identity information was compromised. Report the listing to the platform, the FTC, local law enforcement, and the state attorney general or consumer-protection office.</p>

<h2>Distinguish a scam from a dispute</h2>
<p>A verified landlord can still violate a lease or housing law. Preserve evidence and use state tenant, fair-housing, legal-aid, or court resources for a genuine rental dispute. Do not retaliate, withhold rent, or abandon a lease based only on online advice; remedies are jurisdiction-specific.</p>

<hr><h2>Official sources</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/rental-listing-scams">Federal Trade Commission: rental listing scams</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://reportfraud.ftc.gov/">Federal Trade Commission: ReportFraud</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.identitytheft.gov/">Federal Trade Commission: IdentityTheft.gov</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.usa.gov/state-attorney-general">USAGov: state attorneys general</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. Verify independently before paying or transmitting sensitive information.</em></p>
