        'Roommate Agreements: What to Include' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A roommate agreement is a written plan for how people sharing a home will handle money, space, chores, guests, and problems. It can prevent misunderstandings, but it normally does not replace or modify the lease unless the landlord formally agrees. Every roommate must still understand their direct obligations to the landlord.</p>

<h2>Start with the lease structure</h2>
<p>Record who is on the lease, who is a guarantor, whether leases are individual or joint, and what happens if one person does not pay. Under some joint leases, one tenant may be pursued for the full amount. Do not promise subletting, pets, extra occupants, or alterations that the lease prohibits.</p>

<h2>Divide money precisely</h2>
<p>List each person’s rent share, deposit contribution, utilities, internet, parking, furnishings, and shared supplies. State due dates, payment method, account holder, how variable bills are divided, and what happens after a late payment or fee. Decide how refunds, utility credits, damages, and replacement costs will be allocated and documented.</p>

<h2>Set rules for shared life</h2>
<p>Discuss quiet hours, study and sleep schedules, cleaning, food, dishes, temperature, security, smoking, alcohol, and use of shared property. Assign recurring chores or establish a rotation. Decide whether groceries are individual or shared and how refrigerator and cabinet space is divided. Specific expectations are easier to follow than “be considerate.”</p>

<h2>Address guests, partners, and privacy</h2>
<p>Set advance-notice expectations, overnight frequency, shared-space use, and responsibility for guest behavior within the lease. Agree not to enter bedrooms, use belongings, share access codes, post images, or disclose private information without permission. A roommate agreement must not authorize harassment, discrimination, surveillance, or unlawful conduct.</p>

<h2>Plan for safety and access</h2>
<p>Record emergency contacts if each person freely agrees, how to report repairs, who may contact management, and what to do about lost keys or lockouts. Discuss allergies, accessibility, service or assistance animals, and emergency needs respectfully without requiring unnecessary medical disclosure. Lease and fair-housing processes may control accommodation issues.</p>

<h2>Prepare for change</h2>
<p>Include a process for schedule changes, new roommates, study abroad, withdrawal, graduation, conflict, or early move-out. A departing roommate should not assume that finding a replacement ends lease liability. Require landlord approval where the lease does and document any release, assignment, or sublease separately.</p>

<h2>Use and update the agreement</h2>
<p>Meet before move-in, write the decisions in plain language, and have everyone sign and retain a copy. Revisit it after the first few weeks and when circumstances change. Use a calm check-in or mediation process before conflict becomes urgent. For threats or immediate danger, contact emergency services or appropriate campus support rather than relying on the agreement.</p>

<hr><h2>Official reference points</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://www.usa.gov/tenant-rights">USAGov: leases and state tenant-rights help</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.hud.gov/stat/fheo/rights-obligations">HUD: fair-housing rights and obligations</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.usa.gov/avoid-eviction">USAGov: housing and legal-aid help</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. This is general information, not legal advice. A roommate agreement does not cancel duties under the lease or applicable law.</em></p>
