        'How College Meal Plans Work' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A college meal plan is a prepaid dining arrangement tied to a school term. Plans may use meal swipes, points or dining dollars, declining-balance funds, guest passes, or a combination. Names are not standardized, so compare actual access, expiration, and price rather than choosing by the plan label.</p>

<h2>Understand the plan’s units</h2>
<p>A swipe may provide entry to an all-you-care-to-eat dining hall or a defined exchange at a retail location. Dining dollars usually spend like a restricted campus balance. Guest passes may be separate. Ask which locations, days, meal periods, and menu items accept each unit and whether mobile ordering changes the rules.</p>

<h2>Calculate cost per usable meal</h2>
<p>Divide the plan’s total charge—after identifying mandatory fees—by the number of meals you realistically will use, not merely the advertised swipes. An “unlimited” plan has no meaningful per-swipe count, so compare it with your schedule and dining-hall access. Include groceries and meals purchased during weekends, breaks, internships, or late hours.</p>

<h2>Check expiration and rollover</h2>
<p>Some swipes reset weekly; others last through the term. Dining dollars may roll between terms but expire at year-end, or may not roll at all. Determine whether unused value is refundable, transferable, or forfeited and what happens after withdrawal, graduation, housing cancellation, or a plan change.</p>

<h2>Match the plan to your routine</h2>
<p>Map class, work, practice, lab, and commute times against location hours. Consider how often you leave campus, cook, travel home, or need breakfast and late-night food. First-year or residential students may be required to buy certain plans, while commuters may have smaller options. Note the deadline and fee for changing plans.</p>

<h2>Plan for dietary and disability needs</h2>
<p>Review menus, ingredient and allergen procedures, cross-contact limits, religious or cultural options, and access when dining facilities are closed. Speak with dining and accessibility services before purchase when a disability affects food access. Ask for an individualized review and effective alternatives rather than assuming an allergy label or preference creates one automatic solution.</p>

<h2>Understand billing and aid</h2>
<p>A required plan may appear on the student account; optional funds may be purchased separately. Housing and food can be components of the school’s cost of attendance, but aid amounts and disbursement timing vary. A larger aid budget is not free money, and loans used for food must be repaid. Ask student accounts about refunds and financial aid about enrollment changes.</p>

<h2>Track usage early</h2>
<p>Check the balance every week. If use is too fast, identify lower-cost options before funds run out. If use is too slow, learn whether eligible locations, guest passes, or a permitted plan downgrade can prevent waste. Campus food pantries or basic-needs offices may assist students experiencing food insecurity regardless of plan.</p>

<hr><h2>Official reference points</h2>
<ul>
  <li><a href="https://studentaid.gov/articles/first-time-college-students/">Federal Student Aid: college food and living expenses</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.fns.usda.gov/snap/students">USDA: SNAP rules for students</a></li>
  <li><a href="https://www.myplate.gov/web/eat-healthy/healthy-eating-budget">USDA MyPlate: healthy eating on a budget</a></li>
</ul>
<p><em>Last reviewed: August 16, 2026. Your school’s dining contract, calendar, accessibility process, and refund policy control.</em></p>
