        'Eating on a Student Budget' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A workable food budget begins with the money and cooking access you actually have. Plan a small set of meals around your schedule, compare unit prices, and reduce waste before chasing complicated recipes. If you are skipping meals or running out of food, use campus and public support early.</p>

<h2>Set a weekly amount</h2>
<p>Start with monthly food money after fixed bills, then divide it by the number of weeks until the next income or aid payment. Separate groceries, campus dining, takeout, coffee, and delivery fees so one category does not quietly consume the rest. Keep a small buffer for exam weeks or schedule changes.</p>

<h2>Plan from what you have</h2>
<p>Check the refrigerator, freezer, pantry, and meal-plan balance before shopping. Choose several breakfasts, lunches, dinners, and snacks that reuse ingredients. Plan to eat perishable food first and freeze safe leftovers. Include fast options for busy days so convenience does not always mean delivery.</p>

<h2>Shop by unit price and usable quantity</h2>
<p>Compare the price per ounce, pound, or serving rather than package price alone. Store brands, frozen or canned produce, beans, lentils, oats, eggs, rice, pasta, and seasonal items can stretch a budget, subject to dietary needs. Bulk is cheaper only when you can store and use it before spoilage.</p>

<h2>Use campus dining strategically</h2>
<p>Know when swipes and dining dollars reset or expire. Carry a snack when permitted, use the locations already included in your plan, and avoid paying twice for a meal you prepaid. Follow dining rules about takeout and containers. If the plan does not meet disability-related needs, contact dining and accessibility services.</p>

<h2>Protect health and food safety</h2>
<p>Aim for variety across food groups within your needs rather than pursuing perfection. Use clean water and preparation surfaces, refrigerate perishables promptly, and cook foods to safe temperatures. Do not save money by eating spoiled food or ignoring a severe allergy. Students with medical nutrition needs should use qualified healthcare advice.</p>

<h2>Check support programs</h2>
<p>Campus food pantries, meal-swipe programs, emergency grants, community food banks, and basic-needs offices may help. SNAP has special rules for many students enrolled at least half time; qualifying students generally must meet an exemption and all other eligibility rules. Apply through the state where you live and rely on the current state determination, not social-media eligibility claims.</p>

<h2>Use a simple weekly routine</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Inventory food and dining balances.</li>
  <li>Plan meals around schedule and perishability.</li>
  <li>Shop with a list and compare unit prices.</li>
  <li>Prepare one or two flexible staples.</li>
  <li>Review spending and waste before the next trip.</li>
</ol>

<hr><h2>Official sources</h2>
