        'Commuting to College' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A reliable college commute is a schedule, cost, and backup-plan problem—not just a distance. Test the actual trip at class times, include the walk from parking or transit, and build enough margin for weather, congestion, missed connections, and campus events.</p>

<h2>Map the door-to-seat trip</h2>
<p>Time travel from home to the classroom, including transfers, parking search, shuttle waits, security, and walking. Check the last bus or train after evening classes. A 20-minute drive can become much longer at peak hours or when campus lots fill. Review accessible routes and paratransit eligibility if relevant.</p>

<h2>Build the full budget</h2>
<p>For a car, include fuel, parking permit, tolls, insurance, registration, maintenance, repairs, depreciation or payments, and citations—not only gasoline. For transit, include passes, transfers, first- and last-mile costs, and occasional rideshare. Compare student discounts and whether the school already includes a transit pass in fees.</p>

<h2>Design the class schedule intentionally</h2>
<p>When possible, group classes into fewer campus days without creating an exhausting sequence. Leave time for office hours, tutoring, meals, study groups, and advising rather than arriving only for class. Verify that a short connection works between distant buildings. Online sections may reduce trips but can add technology and self-management demands.</p>

<h2>Create productive campus time</h2>
<p>Identify a commuter lounge, library, lockers, microwaves, refrigerators, charging outlets, quiet study areas, and places open between early and late classes. Pack weather gear, medication, food, water, chargers, and materials for the entire day. Joining one recurring activity can make campus feel less transactional.</p>

<h2>Have at least two backup routes</h2>
<p>Save transit alerts, campus shuttle and parking pages, roadside or repair contacts, and a trusted emergency ride. Know the school’s severe-weather and attendance rules. If transportation fails, notify the instructor promptly, but do not assume a commute problem automatically excuses an absence.</p>

<h2>Protect enrollment and finances</h2>
<p>Transportation is commonly included as an estimated component of cost of attendance, but it is not necessarily a direct payment or enough to cover your actual vehicle. Ask financial aid about documented special circumstances rather than taking additional loans without reviewing repayment cost. A longer commute should be included when comparing college offers.</p>

<h2>Reassess after two weeks</h2>
<p>Track real travel time, spending, missed connections, fatigue, and study time. Adjust departure, parking location, transit pass, carpool, or future course schedule. If the route creates a disability-related barrier, contact accessibility services about effective access options; transportation beyond campus may involve separate providers and eligibility processes.</p>

