        'Do You Need a Car in College?' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A car is useful only if it solves transportation needs that walking, biking, campus shuttles, public transit, carpooling, occasional rentals, or rideshare cannot solve at lower total cost. Decide from a typical month of trips, not from the idea that every college student eventually needs a vehicle.</p>

<h2>List required trips</h2>
<p>Map housing, classes, work, clinical or student-teaching sites, groceries, healthcare, caregiving, religious services, and trips home. Note time of day, frequency, passengers, cargo, disability access, and weather. Ask whether the program assigns placements outside transit range and whether students receive advance notice.</p>

<h2>Test car-free options</h2>
<p>Review campus shuttle maps, local transit, student passes, safe walking and biking routes, grocery delivery, carpools, and weekend service. Check the first and last trip, not just midday frequency. Price a realistic mix—for example, transit plus two rideshares a month—against the entire cost of owning a car.</p>

<h2>Calculate total vehicle cost</h2>
<p>Include purchase price or payment, interest, insurance, registration and taxes, campus and residential parking, fuel or charging, maintenance, tires, inspections, tolls, depreciation, and an emergency repair reserve. Insurance can change by address, driver, vehicle, and use; get a quote before buying. Do not use the financial-aid transportation allowance as a spending target.</p>

<h2>Check campus restrictions</h2>
<p>Some campuses restrict first-year permits, assign remote lots, prohibit overnight parking, or have waitlists. A permit may not guarantee a space. Review snow emergencies, street cleaning, event closures, towing, storage during breaks, and residential lease rules. Confirm whether a vehicle must be registered locally.</p>

<h2>If buying a vehicle</h2>
<p>Set a price that leaves room for taxes, immediate maintenance, and insurance. Get an independent mechanical inspection and review title, ownership, financing, and history through reliable sources. Use NHTSA’s VIN or license-plate lookup for unrepaired safety recalls and compare safety ratings. A recall lookup is not a substitute for a mechanical inspection or title check.</p>

<h2>Plan for breakdowns and safety</h2>
<p>Keep maintenance current, know roadside options, and maintain a backup route for exams and work. Do not drive when impaired, dangerously tired, or in conditions beyond your ability. Store valuables out of sight, follow parking rules, and never leave someone dependent on a ride without communicating a failure promptly.</p>

<h2>Make a semester decision</h2>
<p>Try the likely no-car routine during orientation or the first weeks if practical. Track time and spending. A car may be unnecessary on a dense campus but essential for a rural clinical placement; the answer can change by semester. Selling, storing, or bringing a family vehicle also has costs and insurance consequences.</p>

